// The Demo of Rabenstein: an arc_image demonstration.
// part of Arcturus, a programming language and compiler for the Infocom Z-machine.
// Copyright (c) 2026, Stefan Vogt.
// https://github.com/ByteProject/Arcturus
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT THIS IS
// ============================================================================
// A four-room walk that exercises EVERYTHING arc_image does, so it doubles as
// the interpreter author's test game: traversal between pictured rooms, a
// room with no picture (the band must clear), darkness with its own scene,
// and a picture that changes on an event in the same room, repeatably. One
// room, the library, carries three of the tests at once: it is dark until
// light arrives, and its curtains change its picture in place. A room
// carries a picture that an aware interpreter
// draws and every standard interpreter ignores, so the compiled story stays a
// fully conformant Z-machine v5 file. The art is the Amiga/ST scene work from
// The Curse of Rabenstein, in DAAD mode (320x96).
//
// ============================================================================
// THE TESTER'S WALKTHROUGH (the expected band, step by step)
// ============================================================================
// An interpreter that renders arc_image should show exactly this:
//
//     command                      the band shows
//     (start) Forsaken Path        picture 8
//     NORTH   Churchyard           picture 1
//     NORTH   Open Lawn            NO picture: the band CLEARS (draw id 0)
//     NORTH   Library, dark        picture 21 (the all-black darkness scene)
//     SOUTH, SOUTH  Churchyard     0 on the lawn, then 1 again
//     TAKE LANTERN, LIGHT LANTERN
//     NORTH, NORTH  Library, lit   0 on the lawn, then 17 (the reveal)
//     CLOSE CURTAINS               picture 20, the SAME room repaints at once
//     OPEN CURTAINS                picture 17 again (repeatable, both ways)
//
// And two things that must NOT happen: LOOK in any room must not reload or
// flicker the band (the library redraws only when the picture actually
// changes), and none of this may disturb a text-only interpreter, where the
// same file plays as pure prose.
//
// ============================================================================
// HOW arc_image WORKS
// ============================================================================
// A picture is named by its id, a resource slot. The id IS the identifier
// every target shares: on a modern system an interpreter loads picture <id>;
// a retro build loads the same slot in the machine's own format (21.CPC,
// ARC21.TR4). There is no name table to carry down. Write the id as a plain
// number (arc_image 8) or, as here, a constant that folds to one:
//
//     constant scene_path = 8
//     ...
//         arc_image scene_path
//
// Cosmos reads the property on room entry, behind a capability guard the story
// crosses only on an interpreter that advertises picture support, and draws the
// picture. That guard is the whole safety story: an interpreter only decodes
// bytes its control flow reaches, so on Frotz (and in Actaea's text modes) the
// draw is never reached and the game plays as pure text. Id 0 means "no
// picture" and clears the band; a room without arc_image clears it too, so the
// picture always matches the room.
//
// A picture can CHANGE at run time: `change here.arc_image to <id>` in a
// handler repaints the band the same turn (the library curtains below), and a
// DARK room shows the picture named by the game-wide constant arc_image_dark
// instead of its own, because darkness is a scene too (the library below, and
// picture 21 is exactly that: all black).
//
// Two picture shapes exist, each a whole number of 8-pixel text rows tall so
// the status bar sits flush beneath the band. You pick one game-wide with a
// constant named arc_mode (its value is the band height in text rows):
//     Infocom mode   320x72   9 rows    the upper third (the Arthur look)
//     DAAD mode      320x96   12 rows   the upper half  (the Rabenstein look)
// The interpreter sizes the band from arc_mode, never by measuring a picture,
// so it lays out the screen (and, on an 8-bit machine, its memory) before any
// picture is loaded. A modern interpreter integer-scales the picture to the
// window, crisp for pixel art at any font size; pixel art looks best.
//
// ============================================================================
// THE PICTURES: THE .arcres FILE
// ============================================================================
// The pictures live beside the story, never inside it, so the .z5 stays a
// plain story file. They travel as an .arcres pack: one file, a zip of the
// numbered PNGs keyed by id, that the interpreter reads automatically when it
// sits next to the story. This example ships rabenstein.arcres, which holds
// pictures 1, 8, 17, 20, and 21. It was built with the arcimg tool:
//
//     arcimg pack 1.png 8.png 17.png 20.png 21.png -o rabenstein.arcres
//
// arcimg also prepares art that is not already the right size (cropping and
// scaling a photo or a wrong-sized image to a mode), and lists a pack:
//
//     arcimg prep opening.jpg --id 8 --mode daad -o .   // -> 8.png at 320x96
//     arcimg info rabenstein.arcres                     // list the pack
//
// ============================================================================
// RUNNING IT
// ============================================================================
//     arcc rabenstein.storyarc -o rabenstein.z5    // compile (a plain z5 file)
//     actaea rabenstein.z5                          // play, pictures and all
//
// Actaea is the reference interpreter; the retro interpreters (the TRS-80
// Model 4 was the first adopter-built one to draw the band) load the same
// slots from their own picture files. The very same rabenstein.z5 also
// plays, as pure text, on Frotz and in Actaea's --console and --headless
// modes: the pictures are simply absent there.

game
    title "The Demo of Rabenstein"
    headline "An arc_image demonstration"
    author "Stefan Vogt"
    start opening

// The picture mode, game-wide: 12 is DAAD mode (320x96, the upper half), which
// is what this art is drawn for. The interpreter sizes the band from this, not
// from the picture, so it never has to load a picture to lay out the screen.
constant arc_mode = 12

// The scenes, one constant per resource slot.
constant scene_path = 8
constant scene_church = 1
constant scene_library = 17
constant scene_library_shut = 20

// Darkness is a scene too: a dark room shows THIS picture, never a stale
// leftover from the last lit room. Picture 21 is all black, on every target.
constant arc_image_dark = 21

summon.statusline

// on start runs before the banner (the Infocom order), so the game can set up
// its Z-machine colours before a word is printed.
on start
    zcolor.font white
    zcolor.background black
    zcolor.statusline red
    zcolor.input red

room opening
    name "Forsaken Path"
    desc "You are standing on a path deep in the Black Forest, extending
          further north from here. On a night like this, the trees cast eerie
          shadows and people disappear without ever being seen again."
    arc_image scene_path
    north church

room church
    name "Churchyard"
    desc "A small stone church stands here, its windows dark and its door
          ajar. The path leads back south, and a gate opens north onto the
          manor lawn."
    arc_image scene_church
    south opening
    north lawn

thing lantern in church
    name "storm lantern"
    words lantern, storm, lamp
    desc "A storm lantern someone left on a gravestone, as if they knew."
    switchable
    lit false

    on switch_on
        now self is lit
        say "The lantern catches with a soft hiss."

    on switch_off
        now self is not lit
        say "The flame gutters out, and the dark leans in."

// The clear test: this room has NO arc_image, so an aware interpreter must
// clear the band (the draw of id 0) the moment you arrive.
room lawn
    name "Open Lawn"
    desc "A stretch of open lawn under the night sky, too dark and too plain
          for any picture at all. The churchyard lies south; the manor's
          library door stands north."
    south church
    north library

// Three tests in one room. DARKNESS: without light this room is dark and
// the band shows arc_image_dark (the all-black 21); bring the lit lantern
// and the room's own picture appears (the reveal). THE EVENT: the curtains
// change the picture IN PLACE, same room, same turn, repeatably.
room library
    name "Library"
    desc "Shelves to the ceiling, and moonlight through a tall window with
          heavy curtains. The lawn lies back south."
    arc_image scene_library
    lit false
    south lawn

thing curtains in library
    name "heavy curtains"
    words curtains, curtain, drapes
    scenery
    pluribus
    openable
    open
    desc "Heavy velvet curtains, drawn back from the moonlit window."

    on close
        if self is not open
            say "They are already drawn."
            stop
        now self is not open
        change here.arc_image to scene_library_shut
        say "You draw the curtains, and the moonlight dies out of the room."

    on open
        if self is open
            say "They already stand open."
            stop
        now self is open
        change here.arc_image to scene_library
        say "You pull the curtains back, and the moon reclaims the shelves."
